            <ArticleTitle>Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm of the pancreas: Management and long-term outcome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1056-1060</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0748-7983(17)30346-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejso.2017.02.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm (SPN) of pancreas is a rare pancreatic neoplasm with a low metastatic potential. Our aim was to study the clinical-pathological characteristics, and long-term outcome of this tumor.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Rretrospective single center study of patients operated for SPN of pancreas. Clinical and pathological data were collected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">From 1995 to 2016, 1320 patients underwent pancreatic resection. SPN was confirmed in 32 cases (2.46%), including 29 (90.6%) female and three (9.4%) male, with a mean age of 28.4 ± 12.2 years. SPN was the most common pathology among female patients under age of 40 (72.4%). Abdominal pain was the most frequent presenting symptom (48%), whereas none of the patients presented with jaundice. Mean tumor diameter was 5.9 cm (range, 0.9-14 cm). All patients underwent margin-negative surgical resection. Two patients demonstrated gross malignant features, including liver metastases at presentation (n = 1), and adjacent organ and vascular invasion (n = 1). Microscopic malignant features were present in thirteen patients (40.6%). Recurrence occurred in the retroperitoneal lymph nodes (n = 1, 7 years post resection) and in the liver (n = 2, 1 and 5 years post resection). Mean follow-up was 49.2 months (range, 1-228 months). Five and 10-year disease-free survival was 96.5% and 89.6% respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">SPNs are low-grade tumors with a good prognosis. Margin-negative surgical resection is curative in most patients. However, almost 15% of patients demonstrate malignant features including invasion of adjacent organs or metastatic disease. Patients with malignant disease are still expected to have long survival, and aggressive surgical approach is advocated.</AbstractText>
